---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ProductFrame from "../components/ProductFrame.astro";
const releases = "https://github.com/ShivankK26/Brainlog/releases/latest";
---
<Base>

<!-- ================= HERO ================= -->
<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="pill"><b>New</b> Memory for Claude Code and Cursor over MCP</span>
    <h1>It's your second brain.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Brainlog remembers everything you saw, read, and promised on your computer — then answers questions about it. All of it stays on your machine.</p>
    <div class="cta"><a class="btn p lg" href="#download">Download for macOS</a><a class="btn lg" href="#how">See how it works</a></div>
    <div class="os">Also for Windows and Linux · Free for individuals</div>

    <ProductFrame />
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= MEMO ================= -->
<section class="memo" id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="big">You already have a second brain. It's called your computer. <em>It just doesn't remember.</em></div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Every day you read hundreds of pages, skim thousands of messages, and make promises in passing. By Friday, most of it is gone — not deleted, just unreachable. The thread you meant to follow. The pricing table you glanced at. The branch you abandoned and can't remember why.</p>
      <p>Brainlog runs quietly on your machine and keeps the text of what you see: window titles, pages, messages, terminal output. <b>No screenshots, no cloud, no account.</b> A small local model turns that stream into people, projects, and open loops, so you can ask a question in plain language and get back the moment — what was on screen, and what you did next.</p>
      <p><b>It's the memory your computer should have shipped with.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HOW ================= -->
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">How it works</div>
    <h2 class="h2">Capture, understand, recall. Entirely on your machine.</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div><div class="n">1 — Capture</div><h3>Text, not screenshots</h3><p>Brainlog reads the accessibility tree and window metadata as you work. Only text is kept, only for 30 days, and never from apps or sites you block.</p></div>
      <div><div class="n">2 — Understand</div><h3>A local model connects the dots</h3><p>People, projects, repos, and commitments are extracted on-device and linked to the moments they came from. This graph is small, encrypted, and yours.</p></div>
      <div><div class="n">3 — Recall</div><h3>Ask your past</h3><p>Search by content, person, or time. Get the moment back — with everything else that was open — or let your coding agent ask on your behalf.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="stats">
      <div><div class="l">Data leaving your machine</div><div class="v">0 bytes</div><div class="d">by design, verifiable</div></div>
      <div><div class="l">Storage for a month</div><div class="v">~400 MB</div><div class="d">text is small</div></div>
      <div><div class="l">Recall latency</div><div class="v">&lt;50 ms</div><div class="d">local SQLite + vectors</div></div>
      <div><div class="l">Setup</div><div class="v">1 click</div><div class="d">no account required</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= FEATURES ================= -->
<section id="search" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="feat">
      <div class="txt">
        <div class="eyebrow">Search</div>
        <h3>"That table I saw in Slack two weeks ago."</h3>
        <p>Spotlight searches file names. Brainlog searches what you actually looked at — and rebuilds the moment around it, so you get the context, not just the hit.</p>
        <ul><li>Search by content, app, person, or time</li><li>See everything that was open at that moment</li><li>Follow what you did next</li></ul>
      </div>
      <div class="vis v-search">
        <div class="in">pricing table in slack<span>⌘K</span></div>
        <div class="r on"><div class="ts">Fri 14:05<br>Slack</div><div>Arjun: can someone review the <mark>pricing table</mark> in the onboarding doc? Numbers look stale.</div></div>
        <div class="r"><div class="ts">Thu 09:20<br>Notion</div><div>Onboarding — week 2: <mark>Pricing table</mark> (Starter $29, Team $79, Enterprise custom)</div></div>
        <div class="also">Also on screen at 14:05<div><b>VS Code · widget.tsx</b><b>Firefox · Onboarding — week 2</b><b>Calendar · Platform sync</b></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feat r">
      <div class="txt">
        <div class="eyebrow">Commitments</div>
        <h3>Never drop a promise.</h3>
        <p>"I'll send it by Friday" is a commitment the moment you type it. Brainlog finds them in your messages, watches for evidence you followed through, and surfaces the ones you're about to miss.</p>
        <ul><li>Extracted from Slack, email, and terminal output</li><li>Closes itself when the work shows up</li><li>Knows who's waiting on you, and who you're waiting on</li></ul>
      </div>
      <div class="vis v-cmt">
        <div class="ls" style="background:none">
          <div class="g">Overdue <span>1</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="st b"></span><span class="id">CMT-14</span><span class="t">Memory-layer spec<small>You → Priya · no spec-like document opened since Wed</small></span><span class="m">Sep 11</span></div>
          <div class="g">Open <span>2</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="st"></span><span class="id">CMT-17</span><span class="t">Review pricing table<small>Arjun → you · you replied "looking"</small></span><span class="m">—</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="st p"></span><span class="id">CMT-15</span><span class="t">Fix vec0 load order<small>You → yourself · branch untouched 3 days</small></span><span class="m">—</span></div>
          <div class="g">Done <span>1</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="st d"></span><span class="id">CMT-12</span><span class="t">Send Ollama setup notes<small>Closed automatically · notes.md attached Mon 17:02</small></span><span class="m">Sep 7</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feat" id="agents">
      <div class="txt">
        <div class="eyebrow">Agents</div>
        <h3>Give your coding agent a memory of your week.</h3>
        <p>Claude Code, Cursor, and Codex connect over a local MCP socket and query the same memory you do. They can leave notes, too — held as proposed until you approve.</p>
        <ul><li>One-line setup, no API keys</li><li>Agents read what you read, nothing you blocked</li><li>Every query and write lands in the audit log</li></ul>
        <div class="logos"><span>Claude Code</span><span>Cursor</span><span>Codex</span><span>Any MCP client</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="vis v-term">
<div class="u">&gt; what was I doing in this repo on Wednesday, and why did I stop?</div>
<div class="t">  ⋯ brainlog.query(repo="second-brain", day="2026-09-09")</div>
<div>  Wednesday afternoon you were on feat/graph-edges. At 15:31</div>
<div>  migration 0004 failed with 'no such module: vec0'. You read</div>
<div>  better-sqlite3 #742, then switched to a Slack DM with Priya</div>
<div>  and didn't come back. The branch has been idle since.</div>
<div>&nbsp;</div>
<div>  You also promised Priya a spec by Friday. It's overdue.</div>
<div>  Want me to draft one from the graph-edges work?</div>
<div>&nbsp;</div>
<div class="u">&gt; yes, and remember: load vec0 before migrate</div>
<div class="m">  Saved as proposed — approve in Brainlog to keep it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feat r" id="privacy">
      <div class="txt">
        <div class="eyebrow">Privacy</div>
        <h3>Everything you saw. Nothing you sent.</h3>
        <p>Brainlog is local-first the way a notebook is: there's no server to trust because there's no server. It's a log of your day, kept where your day happens.</p>
        <ul><li>Text only — screenshots are never written to disk</li><li>AES-256-GCM at rest, key in your OS keychain</li><li>API bound to localhost; block any app or domain</li><li>Raw text expires after 30 days; the graph is yours to keep</li></ul>
      </div>
      <div class="vis v-lock">
        <div class="l"><span>Screenshots<small>Images never written to disk</small></span><b><i style="background:var(--red)"></i>Never</b></div>
        <div class="l"><span>Cloud sync<small>No account, no upload</small></span><b><i style="background:var(--red)"></i>Off</b></div>
        <div class="l"><span>Blocked apps &amp; domains<small>1Password, banking, personal mail</small></span><b>5 rules</b></div>
        <div class="l"><span>Encryption at rest<small>AES-256-GCM · per-install key</small></span><b><i style="background:var(--green)"></i>On</b></div>
        <div class="l"><span>Agent access to sensitive content<small>Credentials, finance, others' DMs</small></span><b><i style="background:var(--red)"></i>Blocked</b></div>
        <div class="l"><span>Audit log<small>Every read and write, exportable</small></span><b><i style="background:var(--green)"></i>On</b></div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= BAND ================= -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>You don't need to take notes. <em>You need to stop losing them.</em></h2>
    <p>Brainlog isn't another place to write things down. It's the memory of everything you already did.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CTA ================= -->
<section class="cta-end" id="download">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Start remembering.</h2>
    <p>Free for individuals. One download, no account, nothing leaves your machine.</p>
    <div class="cta" style="display:flex;gap:10px;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn p lg" href={releases}>Download for macOS</a><a class="btn lg" href={releases}>Windows</a><a class="btn lg" href={releases}>Linux</a></div>
    <div class="os" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:13px;color:var(--t3)">Open source core · MIT · Teams and enterprise policies coming</div>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
